\subsection{Feature Subset Stability}
\label{sec:feature_subset}

To test whether $\Kempirical{=}2$ depends on the specific 15D feature combination, we evaluate random feature subsets of varying size on Pythia-1.4B prompt-chain data (Table~\ref{tab:feature_subset}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{1v2 $\auc$ by feature subset size (Pythia-1.4B). $\Kempirical{=}2$ ($\auc > \theta{=}0.60$) is robust at $\geq$8 features.}
\label{tab:feature_subset}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Subset} & \textbf{Tested} & \textbf{Pass} & \textbf{Min $\auc$} & \textbf{Mean $\auc$} \\
\midrule
15D (full) & 1 & 1 & 0.6262 & 0.6262 \\
10D        & 5 & 5 & 0.6153 & 0.6191 \\
8D         & 5 & 5 & 0.6056 & 0.6159 \\
5D         & 5 & 2 & 0.5856 & 0.5998 \\
3D (top-3) & 1 & 0 & 0.5871 & 0.5871 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

$\Kempirical{=}2$ holds for all subsets of size $\geq$8 (10/10 pass, min $\auc = 0.6056$), becomes fragile at 5D (2/5 pass), and fails at 3D (top-3 surprisal features alone: $\auc = 0.5871 < \theta$).
The boundary does not depend on any specific feature combination but requires sufficient dimensionality ($\geq$8) to capture the multi-faceted distributional shift between generations 1 and 2.

